---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import DataTable from "../../../components/story/DataTable.astro";
import DieselChart from "../../../components/DieselChart.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import StoryState from "../../../components/StoryState.astro";
import StateEvent from "../../../components/StateEvent.astro";
import { readPublishedState, eventPath } from "../../../lib/state.mjs";
import { event, prices, release, marchSoFar, shortfalls, usShare, ledgerRows } from "../../../data/fuel-reserves-2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels.mjs";
const state = readPublishedState().events.get("event-fuel-reserves-2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels");
if (!state) throw new Error("Missing publication selection: event-fuel-reserves-2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels");
if (state.status === "merged") return Astro.redirect(eventPath(state.survivor), 301);
const markers = [
  { date: "2026-02-28", label: "War with Iran", record: "euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08", passage: "attacks against Iran on 28 February" },
  { peak: true, anchor: "end" },
  { date: "2026-10-02", label: "G7 release", anchor: "end", record: "govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02", passage: "coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months" },
];
---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/fuel-reserves/">Fuel reserves</a><span>October 2, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Fuel reserves &middot; October 2, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updatedLabel}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#diesel">Diesel since the war</a></li>
          <li><a href="#owed">Who still owes barrels</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sides">The sides</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The leaders of the G7 agreed on October 2 to release {release.barrels} barrels of oil from emergency stocks over {release.months}, with a large release of diesel in the first {release.dieselDays}. The barrels were already promised. The G7's statement presents them as carrying out a pledge governments made in March, after the war with Iran began, to release {release.marchTotal} barrels, and about {marchSoFar.unreleased} barrels of that pledge are still unreleased.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments" /><Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="coordinated release of 400 million barrels" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="would bring approximately 100 million barrels to the market" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">The White House says Trump "successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel from its strategic reserves," and that this will bring diesel prices down for Americans.<Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="President Trump successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel from its strategic reserves within the next four months." /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="This will increase supply and bring diesel prices down for Americans." /> The G7 statement names no amount of diesel, and it has "G7 members and partners" releasing, which includes the United States, Japan and Canada, not Europe alone.<Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="They did not provide a breakdown of the volumes of crude, diesel and other products to be released" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="within the first 20 days by G7 members and partners" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="The meeting was attended by President Donald Trump of the United States, Chancellor Friedrich Merz of Germany, Prime Minister Mark Carney of Canada" /> The meeting was France's. President Emmanuel Macron said on September 18 that he would convene the G7 on oil stocks, and Trump posted that Europe had "just agreed" moments before the statement came out.<Cite s="elysee-macron-2026-09-18" passage="je convoquerai un G7 pour avancer sur une meilleure coordination sur les niveaux de stock" /><Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="Moments before the statement was released, US President Donald Trump said in a social media post that Europe had" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Fuel is where the market is tightest. Crude exports from the Middle East have recovered, Fatih Birol, head of the International Energy Agency, said on October 2, while "refined-product flows remain severely constrained," and Energy Secretary Chris Wright said U.S. refiners are already "running at record highs."<Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="refined products, where market tightness is now most acute" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="While crude oil exports from the Middle East have recovered significantly, he said, refined-product flows remain severely constrained" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage="American refiners are running at record highs" /> The U.S. pledge of {release.usMarch} barrels is all crude. Europe's contributions are mostly refined products, and France and Germany hold some of Europe's largest stocks of them.<Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",United States,172.196,172.196,0,0,172.196,0" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products" /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="boast some of the largest reserves of refined oil products in Europe" /> Wright said Europe would announce "new diesel supplies" that would "meaningfully push diesel prices down."<Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage="about new diesel supplies that'll come to the market. That'll meaningfully push diesel prices down." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">Washington had asked for two things. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said Europe should "accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available."<Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="Our European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available" /> It got the first: the governments of the International Energy Agency, which coordinates the emergency stocks of {release.members} countries, backed finishing the March release as soon as possible, diesel first where they can.<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="32 member nations of the International Energy Agency" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="accelerating the oil stock releases announced in the Collective Action of March 2026" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="IEA Members also supported the prioritisation of the release of diesel stocks, to the extent possible" /> Europe has added nothing beyond March for now. Germany says "no additional quantities" as of now, EU officials are holding off "pending a clearer IEA assessment," and France has floated {shortfalls.franceExtra} of diesel without a firm offer.<Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="as of now, no additional quantities" /><Cite s="politico-eu-iea-assessment-2026-10-07" passage="pending a clearer IEA assessment of market conditions and security-of-supply risks" /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="France on Wednesday signaled it could release 2 million barrels of diesel on top of the quantities agreed in March" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The pressure came with talk of a ban on U.S. diesel exports, which supplied about half of the European Union's diesel imports in August. Trump said on September 22, "let's not send out the diesel," and three people close to the talks said France and Germany were told to release diesel or face a possible ban.<Cite s="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" passage="I've said let's not send out the diesel" /><Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="The IEA said the US supplied roughly half of the EU's diesel imports in August." /><Cite s="reuters-diesel-demand-2026-10-01" passage="or face a potential US diesel export ban, said three people close to the discussions" /> Macron said Trump made no threat on the leaders' call, and hours after it Trump ruled out a ban.<Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="Mr Macron said Mr Trump did not threaten to issue an embargo on US diesel" /><Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="he will not authorize an export ban on diesel, hours after G7 countries agreed to release diesel and crude oil from their emergency reserves." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">Diesel is in tight supply. In the U.S. it has gone from {prices.beforeWar} to {prices.oct5} a gallon since the war began.<Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="given the current tightness in diesel markets" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="02/23 3.809" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="2026-Oct 10/05 6.199" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">The agency holds a board meeting next week that is expected to settle who releases what, and how much of it is diesel.<Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="the next scheduled meeting of the IEA Governing Board, which will take place next week" /><Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="is expected to decide the details of the stocks release at an October 14-15 board meeting" /><Cite s="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" passage="including volumes, timing and the type of refined oil products to be released" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="diesel">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Diesel since the war</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">The United States and Israel attacked Iran on February 28, and tanker traffic through the Strait of Hormuz, the Gulf's sea route to the world, nearly stopped.<Cite s="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" passage="attacks against Iran on 28 February and the closure of the Strait of Hormuz" /> On March 11 the agency's members pledged to release {release.marchTotal} barrels.<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="coordinated release of 400 million barrels" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In August the Gulf's net diesel exports were about a quarter of their prewar level, and Ukrainian attacks on Russian refineries had nearly halted Russia's fuel exports.<Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="averaged 390 kb/d in August, just over a quarter of pre-war levels" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="a near-halt to product exports following intensified Ukrainian attacks" /> U.S. diesel went from {prices.beforeWar} a gallon the week before the war to a record {prices.record} on September 21, and was {prices.oct5} on October 5. EU officials say diesel prices in Europe have doubled since late February.<Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="02/23 3.809" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="09/21 6.529" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="2026-Oct 10/05 6.199" /><Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="diesel prices have doubled" /></p>
        <DieselChart source="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" from="2026-01-05" to="2026-10-05" markers={markers} />
      </section>

      <section id="owed">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who still owes barrels</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">By October 2 about {marchSoFar.released} of the March barrels had been released. That would leave {marchSoFar.onPaper}, but some countries released more than they promised, and the countries that fell short still owe about {marchSoFar.unreleased}.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="with some countries having released greater volumes than they initially pledged" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="would bring approximately 100 million barrels to the market" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Governments offer emergency stocks to the market, and companies can take less than is offered. Germany has released {shortfalls.germanyShare} of the oil it promised. It says the market called up its first tranche and sees no need for more, and it attributes the shortfall to weak demand for the barrels.<Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="Of the 2.65 million tons of oil, diesel and jet fuel promised, Germany has so far released only 600,000 tons onto the market." /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Eine erste Tranche dieser für die Freigabe vorgesehenen Mengen wurde mittlerweile von den Marktteilnehmern abgerufen." /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Derzeit wird vom Markt keine weitere Freigabe für erforderlich gehalten." /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="Germany attributes that to weak demand for the barrels" /> Paris has said privately that it has {shortfalls.franceLeft} left.<Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="Paris has indicated privately that it still has 8 million barrels left under the March commitment" /> The United States lends its barrels to oil companies, which repay them with extra later. It has awarded such loans for {usShare.awarded} of its {release.usMarch} barrels; when it offered the last {usShare.offer} in June, companies took about 500,000, and it offered them again on September 29.<Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-03-20" passage="DOE will move forward with an exchange of 45.2 million barrels of crude oil and receive 55 million barrels in return" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels" /><Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="In June, the US offered to loan the last 40 million barrels from that agreement, but later that month revealed that companies only agreed to borrow about 500,000 barrels." /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil" /> Wright said on September 29 that the United States and Japan were delivering while several European countries had released "only a fraction" of their pledges.<Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="several European member countries have released only a fraction of the crude oil and petroleum products they pledged" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The March pledge and what is left of it</strong></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["What is counted", "Barrels"]} rows={ledgerRows} ariaLabel="The March pledge, what has been released, what is still owed and the U.S. share" />
          <p class="chart-source">The agency's counts of October 2 and 7; the U.S. awards are summed from the Energy Department's five award notices, March 20 to June 22.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="would bring approximately 100 million barrels to the market" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-03-20" passage="exchange of 45.2 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-04-10" passage="8.5-million-barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-04-17" passage="exchange 26 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-05-11" passage="approximately 53.3 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-06-22" passage="500,000 barrels" /></p>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The sides</h2>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Washington</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump wrote on October 2 that Europe had agreed to release "a massive amount" of diesel, and Wright called the release European and "the majority diesel."<Cite s="trumpstruth-post-2026-10-02" passage="Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil." /><Cite s="wright-post-2026-10-02" passage="The 100-million-barrel release announced earlier today by European nations" /><Cite s="wright-post-2026-10-02" passage="the majority diesel" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Europe</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The European Commission said: "We fully reject any ban on diesel."<Cite s="france24-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="We fully reject any ban on diesel." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Both sides back the same plan: the barrels go through the agency, with a large diesel release up front, and the G7 countries recommitted to not restricting energy exports to one another.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>"100 million barrels of refined diesel"</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">France's account of the same call says refined products, including diesel, and crude, and the leaders gave no breakdown between them. The White House's fact sheet of October 5 calls all 100 million barrels "refined diesel."<Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="President Trump successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="notamment de diesel et de pétrole brut" /><Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="They did not provide a breakdown of the volumes of crude, diesel and other products to be released" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">The board is expected to meet October 14 and 15 to settle the volumes, the timing and which fuels go out.<Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="is expected to decide the details of the stocks release at an October 14-15 board meeting" /><Cite s="euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08" passage="including volumes, timing and the type of refined oil products to be released" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The White House says the release will bring diesel prices down for Americans. The March release had put about {marchSoFar.released} barrels on the market by October 2, while U.S. diesel climbed to its record {prices.record} on September 21.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="09/21 6.529" /> Ole Hansen, head of commodity strategy at Saxo Bank, said the stress is "no longer crude availability" but "refined product supply, constrained by reduced refinery capacity."<Cite s="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" passage="the main stress in the energy market is no longer crude availability, with Middle East flows recovering, but rather refined product supply, constrained by reduced refinery capacity" /> Neither Germany nor the agency says why demand for Germany's offered stocks was weak while diesel is short. Tenders over the next two weeks "will tell us exactly how much oil and in which location it's going to come out," said Russell Hardy, Vitol's chief executive.<Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="those tenders will tell us exactly how much oil and in which location it's going to come out" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      <StoryState view={state} supporting />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>}
